    {
      "id": "BEQ-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "title": "Averaging ideal divisibility controls the product tail uniformly in the real scale",
      "anchor": "lifts-binary-scalar-average",
      "statement": "Write the scaled scalar average as δ^d times a sum over β∈F× of G(δβ)∏gᵥ(β). For each η>0 one can choose a finite T₀ containing S such that every finite T⊇T₀ gives a truncated normalized sum within η of the full one for all δ>0. Each fixed truncation tends to (∫G)∏_{v∈S}∫gᵥ.",
      "hypotheses": "G is nonnegative, continuous and compactly supported. Factors in S are also nonnegative, continuous and compactly supported. Good factors are nonnegative, continuous away from zero, supported on the local integer ring and have integral one. At nonzero integral arguments they differ from one by at most C/q, and on units by at most C/q², with C chosen nonnegative. The truncated sum requires β integral outside T.",
