      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "Bracket each added local factor at all nonzero arguments by continuous compactly supported tests of integrals arbitrarily close to one. For the infinite product expand in local deviations. After clearing a fixed denominator H, any subset of specified nonunit conditions forces Hβ into the product of those prime ideals. Separated real embedding boxes bound their scaled number by a constant times the inverse ideal norm. Summing the 2^|D| branches bounds the averaged product of deviations by C′∏_{v∈D}2C/qᵥ². Square-norm summability makes the contribution of sets meeting the omitted places small uniformly in δ. Fix the tail before invoking the lattice limit at that finite truncation.",
      "review": "Checked the exclusion of the zero scalar, the two different local error estimates, the extra inverse norm only after averaging, the full uniform quantifier and the order of the two approximations. The count of nonzero lengths in a box scaled by δ has an explicit upper constant (4RH)^d."
    },
